<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faizullah Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1733">1733</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Nawab Faizullah Khan of Rampur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faizullah Khan was a Rohilla Afghan chief, son of Ali Muhammad Khan, who founded the princely state of Rampur. After the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and the British defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> in 1774, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lal-dhang">Treaty of Lal Dhang</a> let him keep Rampur and its surrounding lands. He built up the town and began the collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raza-library">Rampur Raza Library</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faizullah-khan.jpg" alt="Faizullah Khan" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nawab_Faizullah_Khan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Faizullah Khan was the second son of Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohammed-khan">Ali Mohammed Khan</a> and succeeded his elder brother, Nawab Saadullah Khan, as chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a>. He fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> and was granted Shikohabad for his service. In 1774, when the armies of the Vizier Shuja-ud-Daula and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> and many Rohilla chiefs were killed, he escaped with his treasure to the hills, gathered the survivors and was widely accepted as his father's heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After a month's siege in the forest of Lal Dang both sides sought peace, and a treaty mediated by Colonel Champion let him choose the land for his state. He agreed to cut his army from 20,000 to 5,000, to correspond only with the Company or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, and to raise up to 3,000 troops when Awadh went to war. With Awadh reduced to a British puppet after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>'s death, he sought ratification from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> had it ratified in 1778 through Nathaniel Middleton, after taking one lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> for the Company.</p><p>Rampur's cavalry became valuable to the British against raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gujars</a> and outlaw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a>, and in 1779 he sent 2,000 horse to help the British against the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>. Short of funds in the early 1780s, the Company pressed him for more horse than the treaty required, and by the Treaty of Chunar of 1781 Hastings agreed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>'s annexation of Rampur. The Court of Directors judged this illegitimate in February 1783, but Faizullah still paid 15 lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> after demands brought by William Palmer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Balancing refusals with concessions, Faizullah kept Rampur at peace for some 20 years until his death on 17 July 1794. He was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, but his eldest son, Muhammad Ali Khan, took up the Shia creed under the influence of Nawab Asaf ud Daula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, and Rampur became one of the important Shia princely states. He had 18 children by four wives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Faizullah Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the British-backed Nawab of Awadh defeated the Rohillas in 1774, the Rohilla chief <b>Faizullah Khan</b>, son of Ali Muhammad Khan, was allowed to keep Rampur and surrounding lands by the Treaty of Lal Dhang.</blockquote><a href="/state/rampur-state">Rampur State</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of Rohilkhand was annexed to Awadh, while the Rohilla chief <b>Faizullah Khan</b> kept the small state of Rampur.</blockquote><a href="/state/rohilla-state">Rohilla State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Rampur State. Died in Rampur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.79,79.02&amp;year=1772" aria-label="Rampur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6614 1110 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Faizullah Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7064 1380h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7064 1380h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=28.79,79.02&amp;year=1772">What was here in 1772? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
